[SetUp "1"]

{ A threat often depends on cooperation between two attacking pieces. Removing the support may defeat the threat while gaining material, even if the attacking queen stays active. } 1. Qxd6 ( 1. Qh3 { Alternative for comparison; read the explanation before treating it as a recommendation. } 1... Qxh3 2. Nxh3 ) 1... Qxh2+ 2. Kxh2 { Illustrative line. Qxd6 removes the bishop that makes the h2 attack dangerous. If Black blindly continues ...Qxh2+, White can now play Kxh2 because the queen has lost its defender. The line is a test of the old threat, not a recommendation that Black sacrifice its queen. After Qxd6, Black should choose a better reply, but White has already won the bishop and stopped the immediate mating idea. A defensive move can look active because it attacks the foundation of the threat. } *

[Event "Find a Defensive Resource / 07.3B"]
